Go 1.26 and 1.27 include experimental APIs for Single Instruction Multiple Data (SIMD) operations. SIMD is a native feature of many modern CPUs that allows software to perform uniform operations across vectors of data very quickly, such as adding 8 pairs of  values in a single instruction. It can significantly speed up many computationally-intensive tasks, ranging from cryptography to data processing to AI. In fact, Go’s Green Tea garbage collector even makes use of SIMD to accelerate scanning memory for live objects.Prior to these new experimental APIs, the only way to access this functionality from Go was by writing Go assembly. This was only worth it for truly performance-critical compute kernels, which meant plenty of software that could benefit from SIMD simply left a lot of the CPU unused.Go 1.26 introduced a SIMD API for amd64, and Go 1.27 added APIs for arm64 (specifically NEON) and wasm. However, a basic challenge for a SIMD API is the enormous variation between platforms, not simply in what operations they support, but even in how vectors are represented. Some platforms provide fixed-size vectors, typically between 128 bits and 512 bits, while on others the vector size isn’t known at build time and must be queried when the program starts. To provide full access to the breadth of these platforms, these APIs live in an architecture-dependent  package.But Go 1.27 goes beyond these architecture-dependent APIs and introduces an experimental, fully portable, platform- and size-agnostic SIMD interface,
loosely based on Highway for C++.  The goal is to support write-once near-asm-performance “simd” code on platforms with SIMD support, and to provide a competent emulation on those platforms that do not (yet) have SIMD support.  The  package currently supports AVX, AVX2, and AVX512 on amd64, NEON on arm64, and wasm’s SIMD instructions.Motivation: variation among SIMD architecturesSIMD architectures vary in several dimensions.  Some provide a single fixed vector size (wasm, PowerPC, and s390x, 128 bits).  Some provide several fixed vector sizes (amd64, with 128, 256, and 512; loong64 with 128 and 256).  Riscv64 supports vectors of unspecified size between 128 and 65536 bits, though the length is limited to powers of 2.  Arm64 supports one fixed size (128 bits, NEON), and one variable size (128-2048 bits, powers of two only, SVE).  On a given instance of a particular architecture, determining what sizes that particular instance happens to support requires feature checks: amd64, but is it AVX, AVX2, or AVX512?  Arm64, but is it NEON or SVE?  If SVE, how large? Which variant of SVE: SVE, SVE2, or SVE2.1?Different SIMD architectures vary in how they handle vector masking. For vectors, if-then-else across a vector can be implemented with masks; do the operation, but only assign the result (or load, or store) where the mask is “true”.  Some SIMD variants do not provide masks; all operations work across all elements, and “masking” is done with vector bitmasks and vector boolean operations (wasm, AVX, AVX2, NEON).  Some provide special mask registers, with one bit governing operations on one vector element (AVX512 and RVV).  Others (SVE) allocate one bit per vector byte, but the least-significant bit of each element’s mask bits governs masked operations.  AVX2 also supports masked loads and stores, but using a plain vector as the mask, and with the most-significant bit governing the operation.A third source of variation is in the operations themselves.  Each architecture provides its own primitives for rearranging vector elements; some require constant inputs, others support variable inputs.  Different SIMD architectures support different crypto-related operations.  Even basic arithmetic can have varying support; for example wasm lacks comparisons for vectors of 64-bit integers.  Even for a given vector length on a particular architecture, instruction support depends on “features” that must be checked.Even though Go’s architecture-dependent  package was designed to be as uniform as possible across architectures, many of these quirks remain, and make designing, writing, and testing code for multiplatform SIMD onerous.  We could do more in the  package to make the different architectures appear more similar, but we can only go so far without compromising efficiency.The new  package hides these differences by removing fixed-size vectors from the type system, and by only supporting those operations that are in the intersection of all the different platforms, and fills gaps in the intersection with efficient emulation in terms of other SIMD instructions.  The goal is a set of operations that isadequate to support many data processing algorithms that benefit from a vectorized implementation (but are not tied to a particular vector size),is as efficient as assembly language when the source code operations match the underlying hardware,is otherwise emulated as well as possible,and is easy to read and understand (even/especially if an LLM ends up writing the code).On platforms that lack SIMD instructions or that lack support in , all of the operations are emulated,
so that code written using the  package will always run.To use this experimental package, set  at build time, just like using the experimental  package.The  vector types are just capitalized, plural, primitive types, for example  or . Vectors are loaded from and stored to slices, for example:// innerProduct returns the inner product of x and y.
func innerProduct(x, y []float32) float32 {
    var a simd.Float32s
    var i int
    for i = 0; i < len(x)-a.Len()+1; i += a.Len() {
        u := simd.LoadFloat32s(x[i : i+a.Len()])
        v := simd.LoadFloat32s(y[i : i+a.Len()])
        a = u.MulAdd(v, a)
    }
    if i < len(x) {
        u, _ := simd.LoadFloat32sPart(x[i:])
        v, _ := simd.LoadFloat32sPart(y[i:])
        a = u.MulAdd(v, a)
    }
    return sum(a)
}
// sum returns scalar sum of elements of x.
func sum(x simd.Float32s) float32 {
    s := make([]float32, x.Len())
    x.Store(s)
    var r float32
    for _, e := range s {
        r += e
    }
    return r
}
This example also shows one of the limitations of the first experimental release of this package; because there’s no common way to sum across all the elements of a vector, it’s not supported by  in Go 1.27, though  will appear in the next release so  can be replaced with just .SIMD comparisons produce mask values, which are specific to the corresponding vector element width, so that comparisons of  produce , etc., and mask values can be used to select and filter vectors.Supported  package operations as of Go 1.27In this table,  and  are vector types,  is a mask type,  is a scalar type, and  is a width.Package-Level Load / Broadcast FunctionsIfElse(mask MaskWs, y V) VBoolean and vector masking operationsCarrylessMultiplyEven(y V) VCarrylessMultiplyOdd(y V) VIfElse(mask MaskWs, y V) VConvertToFloatW() FloatWsShift and rotate operationsRotateAllLeft(dist uint64) VRotateAllRight(dist uint64) VShiftAllLeft(dist uint64) VShiftAllRight(dist uint64) VZero-cost reshaping operationsReshapeToUint16s() Uint16sReshapeToUint32s() Uint32sReshapeToUint64s() Uint64sIt may happen that the  package is too limited for all parts of a particular application, or that we have not yet provided an adequate emulation for some necessary feature.  For that case, the  package supports transition to and from architecture-specific SIMD.  Each vector type in the  package has a conversion method  returning an .  That  can be type-asserted to one of the architecture-specific types for a platform.  To convert back, use one of the  functions.  For portable code this creates an obligation to write architecture-specific code for each of the platforms, including an emulation.Here’s a complete example for a method/function that is currently missing, but should be added in Go 1.28. Suppose your algorithm needs  (which  in Go 1.27 lacks).  Rather than rewriting the entire algorithm for each platform, it’s possible to just implement the missing operation.First, for amd64, which lacks the instruction for AVX and AVX2, but not AVX512://go:build goexperiment.simd && amd64
package simd_test
import (
    "simd"
    "simd/archsimd"
)
var popcnt4x16 = [16]int8{0, 1, 1, 2, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1, 2, 2, 3, 2, 3, 3, 4}
var popcnt4x32 = [32]int8{
    0, 1, 1, 2, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1, 2, 2, 3, 2, 3, 3, 4,
    0, 1, 1, 2, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1, 2, 2, 3, 2, 3, 3, 4,
}
// OnesCount returns the number of one bits for each element.
func OnesCount(v simd.Int8s) simd.Int8s {
    switch x := v.ToArch().(type) {
    case archsimd.Int8x16:
        lut := archsimd.LoadInt8x16Array(&popcnt4x16)
        mask0f := archsimd.BroadcastInt8x16(0x0f)
        lo := x.And(mask0f)
        hi := x.ToBits().ReshapeToUint16s().ShiftAllRight(4).
                ReshapeToUint8s().BitsToInt8().And(mask0f)
        return simd.Int8sFromArch(lut.PermuteOrZero(lo).
                Add(lut.PermuteOrZero(hi)))
    case archsimd.Int8x32:
        lut := archsimd.LoadInt8x32Array(&popcnt4x32)
        mask0f := archsimd.BroadcastInt8x32(0x0f)
        lo := x.And(mask0f)
        hi := x.ToBits().ReshapeToUint16s().ShiftAllRight(4).
                ReshapeToUint8s().BitsToInt8().And(mask0f)
        return simd.Int8sFromArch(lut.PermuteOrZeroGrouped(lo).
                Add(lut.PermuteOrZeroGrouped(hi)))
    case archsimd.Int8x64:
        return simd.Int8sFromArch(x.OnesCount())
    default:
        // GODEBUG=simd=0 emulation
        return OnesCountEmulated(v)
    }
}
The interface conversion and type switch look like they should be inefficient, but the compiler-side implementation of  specializes code and optimizes away the type switch.NEON and Wasm both support , so their implementation is much simpler, though it still uses  and .//go:build goexperiment.simd && (wasm || arm64)
package simd_test
import (
    "simd"
    "simd/archsimd"
)
// OnesCount returns the number of one bits for each element.
func OnesCount(v simd.Int8s) simd.Int8s {
    // TODO when SVE is added, this won't work
    switch x := v.ToArch().(type) {
    case archsimd.Int8x16:
        return simd.Int8sFromArch(x.OnesCount())
    default:
        // GODEBUG=simd=0 emulation
        return OnesCountEmulated(v)
    }
}
Don’t forget that some people don’t have hardware SIMD support://go:build goexperiment.simd && !(amd64 || wasm || arm64)

package simd_test
import (
    "simd"
)
// OnesCount returns the number of one bits for each element.
func OnesCount(v simd.Int8s) simd.Int8s {
    return OnesCountEmulated(v)
}
And to complete the exercise, a separate emulation function shared as a fallback across all implementations://go:build goexperiment.simd
package simd_test
import (
    "simd"
)
// OnesCountEmulated returns the number of one bits for each element.
func OnesCountEmulated(v simd.Int8s) simd.Int8s {
    a := [2]uint64{}
    v.ToBits().ReshapeToUint64s().Store(a[:])
    a0, a1 := a[0], a[1]
    m1 := uint64(0x5555555555555555)
    m2 := uint64(0x3333333333333333)
    m4 := uint64(0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0f)
    a0 = (a0 & m1) + ((a0 >> 1) & m1)
    a1 = (a1 & m1) + ((a1 >> 1) & m1)
    a0 = (a0 & m2) + ((a0 >> 2) & m2)
    a1 = (a1 & m2) + ((a1 >> 2) & m2)
    a0 = (a0 & m4) + ((a0 >> 4) & m4)
    a1 = (a1 & m4) + ((a1 >> 4) & m4)
    a[0], a[1] = a0, a1
    return simd.LoadUint64s(a[:]).ReshapeToUint8s().BitsToInt8()
}
API intersection and method emulationWhatever operations the  package offers need to run acceptably well on most architectures.  As a first step, any operation that is supported everywhere, can easily be supported on .  This tends to include loads, stores, arithmetic, and comparisons (but not all comparisons!).A naive intersection across SIMD methods from different architectures still leaves plenty of holes.
These are filled by adding emulations to the various architecture-specific  APIs.
These APIs already contain many trivial emulations to simplify life for Go programmers;
signed and unsigned integer addition use the same instruction,
but in the same way that Go supports the  operator for both  and ,
the  package provides both  and ,
even though those compile to the same instruction.
Modern programming languages also don’t expect programmers to know how to implement floating point negation and absolute value with bit fiddling,
so  implements that where necessary, or “emulates” if you look at it just so.There are many emulations that require just 2 or 3 instructions; for example, some architectures support only a same-value shift distance across vector elements, while others support a different shift distance for each vector element.  To support scalar shifting in , we emulate scalar shift with vector shift.  Some architectures lack some unsigned comparisons–these are just signed comparison, plus two XORs with a constant.Not all missing instructions are that simple.  The “carryless multiply” instruction is important to cryptography and CRC checksumming, but it isn’t always supported.  Leaving that out of the  API would prevent its use for some important algorithms.  Therefore, we provide an emulation, and because one important use is in crypto, its run time does not vary depending on its inputs.In other cases, rather than implement a primitive instruction like “add pairs” (also called “horizontal addition”), for the  package in the next release we will provide the higher level operation that add pairs is usually used for, which is sum reduction.  This also helps insulate users from vector-length dependence; even given the hardware instruction for adding pairs, the number of reduction steps depends on the vector length.The constraint of supporting all platforms, including ones that we predict will appear in  within the next year or so, forces a somewhat conservative approach to which methods we add to .  Riscv64, ppc64, s390x, and loong64 all have their own SIMD extensions.On platforms where there is some hardware support, behavior can be modified with , to make it easier to test -using code with various hardware configurations. You can set the  environment variable prior to executing your program.In Go 1.27, levels of SIMD support are roughly described by vector length: means use emulation for SIMD operations even if the hardware support is available. means use 128-bit vectors and their features.  If the features aren’t available, panic immediately. means use 256-bit vectors and their features, if possible. means use 512-bit vectors and their features, if possible. means use 128-bit vectors and their features even if some features are not supported. If unsupported instructions are used, the code will panic, but if they are not it may still run.  An example of this is Raspberry Pi, which supports NEON but lacks PMULL (carryless multiply). means use 256-bit vectors and their features even if some features are not supported. If unsupported instructions are used, the code will panic, but if they are not it may still run.  An example of this is Apple Silicon’s amd64 emulation, which supports AVX2 but not VPCLMULQDQ (again, carryless multiply). means use 512-bit vectors, even if some features are not supported.If you are debugging code that uses , or even just look at a stack trace, you will notice some weird extra types and methods.  The reason is that  is both a package, an internal implementation package, and some AST rewriting in the front end of the compiler.The AST rewrite creates multiple specialized copies of functions, variables, and types that mention  types, where  types are replaced with references to size-specialized types in .  Each of these bridge types is defined as an  type, but with a restricted set of methods.  The specialized functions, variables, and types acquire a suffix of the form , where  is either a vector length (128, 256, or 512) or 0, indicating emulation.  Functions that mention  internally, but not in their signature, are converted to wrappers that switch on the SIMD level detected at program start, and call the appropriate specialized version of that function.  Specialized functions call other specialized functions directly without dispatch overhead (and perhaps with inlining).  This rewrite strategy was chosen as a compromise between code duplication and SIMD performance; the overhead is hoisted as high as necessary to avoid dispatch within SIMD computations, but not higher.  If SIMD dispatch appears “too low” in a computation, a gratuitous mention of a  type will move it upwards, as in this example:func BenchmarkVpsumdSIMD(b *testing.B) {
    // mention "simd" so the benchmark loop calls specialized vpsumd3 directly
    var _ simd.Uint64s
    var w, x, y, z uint64 = ... // magic constants omitted.
    var lo, hi uint64
    for b.Loop() {
        // vpsumd3 does simd stuff, but lacks a simd signature,
        // so that it can be compared with non-SIMD emulations.
        lo, hi = vpsumd3(w, x, y, z)
    }
    sinkLo, sinkHi = lo, hi
}
We plan to publish a blog post describing  in greater detail soon.For Go 1.28, we intend to add SVE support to , and also hope to add that to .  More importantly, we hope to add additional SIMD operations to those that the  package already supports (e.g., OnesCount, mask operations, reduction operations, vector shuffling operations). Go 1.28 will also include a small number of “feature variants” to avoid downgrading all the way to full emulation for platforms that have a hardware vector implementation but just lack one or a few operations, such as Raspberry Pi.

When systems need to receive updates as soon as they happen, there are four common approaches: , , , and .They all solve a similar problem: delivering new information without waiting for the next normal request. But they do it in very different ways, and each is better suited to a different communication pattern.In this article we’ll look at how each one works, its trade-offs, and when to use it.I also created a  on real-time communication patterns with visuals and animations.Subscribe for more such videos!Traditional HTTP is client-driven. The client sends a request, and the server responds. The server does not send anything unless the client asks first.So if the client wants fresh data, one simple option is to  the server every few seconds:That works when updates are infrequent, or when a small delay is acceptable.But real-time applications are different. Updates can happen at any moment, and the client does not know when they will arrive. That creates a trade-off:, and users see delays. If a new message arrives just after a poll, the client has to wait until the next one., and you waste resources on requests that often return no new data.Real-time systems need a more efficient way to receive updates as soon as they become available. Each of the four approaches below solve this problem in a different way.Long Polling works on top of regular HTTP. The main difference is that the server does not always respond right away.Suppose a client wants to know when a new message arrives. It sends a request to the server., the server responds immediately., the server does not return an empty response. It keeps the request open and waits.When a new message arrives, the server sends it as the response. The client then immediately opens another request and waits again.This creates an almost continuous flow of updates, even though each update still uses a separate HTTP request.The biggest advantage of Long Polling is . It uses standard HTTP, so it works with existing browsers, proxies, and load balancers. There is no new protocol to support.The downside is overhead.Every update costs a full request. After each response, the client sends a new request with headers, authentication data, and other metadata. For a small update, that metadata can be larger than the data itself. The server has to keep a request open for every waiting client. At small scale this is fine. With hundreds of thousands of clients, the cost adds up.There is a gap between requests. Between one response and the next request, the client is not listening. An update that arrives in that gap is delayed, and it can be missed entirely if the server does not track what each client has already seen.The usual fix for that last problem is a cursor. The client sends the ID of the last event it received, and the server returns everything after it before it starts waiting.So Long Polling is simple and practical, but it is less efficient for high-frequency updates.Server-Sent Events also use HTTP. But unlike Long Polling, the connection stays open after an update is sent.The client opens one connection, and the server keeps sending new events over it. The client never has to create a new request after each update.On the wire, SSE uses a simple text-based format. Each event is sent as one or more fields such as  and , followed by a blank line:HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/event-stream

id: 41
data: {"type": "notification", "text": "Build finished"}

id: 42
data: {"type": "notification", "text": "New comment on your PR"}A common modern use case of SSE is streaming responses from large language models. Instead of waiting for the entire answer to be generated, the server sends chunks of the response as they become available, all over the same open connection.This makes SSE more efficient than Long Polling for continuous server-to-client updates.Browsers support SSE natively through the  API, and it reconnects on its own if the connection drops:const events = new EventSource('/events');

events.onmessage = (event) => {
  showNotification(JSON.parse(event.data));
};If the server tags events with an , the browser sends the last one it saw in a  header when it reconnects. The server can then resume from that point instead of starting over.The most important thing to understand about SSE is that it only flows . The client cannot send messages back over the same connection. If it needs to send something, it makes a separate HTTP request.That makes SSE a good fit when communication mainly flows in one direction:Progress updates for long-running jobsBut what if both the client and the server need to send data to each other in real time? That is where WebSockets come in.WebSockets are designed for persistent, two-way communication between a client and a server.The connection usually starts as a normal HTTP request. The client asks the server to upgrade the connection to the WebSocket protocol:GET /game HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Upgrade: websocket
Connection: Upgrade
Sec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==
Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13If the server accepts, it replies with . From then on, the connection stays open and both sides can send messages at any time.After the upgrade, data no longer travels as HTTP requests and responses. It travels as lightweight , which carry only a few bytes of framing instead of a full set of HTTP headers.Example: A Multiplayer GameSuppose we are building a multiplayer game.When a player moves, their client immediately sends that update to the server.The server pushes the new position to every other player over their existing WebSocket connections.Nobody has to ask the server whether anything changed.Because the connection is bidirectional, each client keeps sending its own actions while it receives everyone else’s in real time.When WebSockets Make SenseWebSockets are a natural fit when both sides need to communicate frequently, in real time, with low latency. Common examples include:Other highly interactive applications, such as live trading screensThe Cost: Operational ComplexityWebSockets are powerful, but that power comes with more operational complexity.Every connected user keeps a connection open. At scale, that can mean hundreds of thousands, or even millions, of active connections spread across many servers.Suppose a user is connected to server A, but an event for that user is generated on server B. Server B cannot send the event directly to the client. It first needs to get that event to server A, which owns the connection.This usually requires shared infrastructure such as a message broker or pub/sub system that lets WebSocket servers exchange events.Connections will eventually drop. Clients need reconnection logic, servers need to detect dead connections, and deployments need a graceful way to drain existing connections before shutting a server down.So WebSockets are a strong choice for true two-way, low-latency communication, but they are also the most operationally demanding of the four approaches to run at scale.So far, every approach has focused on communication between a client and a server. But real-time events also happen , where one service needs to notify another when something happens.In these cases, keeping a persistent connection open is often unnecessary. That is where  come in.Suppose your application uses a payment provider like Stripe. A customer starts a payment, but it may take several seconds, or even minutes, to complete.Your backend could keep polling the provider to ask whether the payment has finished. But that wastes requests, and the answer is “no” most of the time.Instead, your application gives the payment provider an , such as . When the payment succeeds, the provider sends an HTTP request to that endpoint with the payment details:POST /webhooks/payments HTTP/1.1
Host: shop.example.com
Content-Type: application/json
Webhook-Signature: t=1758700000,v1=5257a869e7ecebeda32affa62cdca3fa51cad7e7

{"id": "evt_1042", "type": "payment.succeeded", "order_id": "ord_881", "amount": 4900}Your server receives the request, reads the event, processes it, and returns .The key difference from SSE and WebSockets is that there is no persistent connection. When an event occurs, the sender makes a normal HTTP request to the receiver. Once the request is handled, the connection can close.This makes Webhooks a good fit for events that happen occasionally and at unpredictable times, such as:Code is pushed to a repository and CI should runAn email is delivered or bouncesA file finishes processingWebhooks sound simple, but reliable delivery needs a few extra considerations.Your server might be down, slow, or return an error when the webhook arrives. The sender should not assume the event was processed. Webhook providers usually retry failed deliveries, often with increasing delays between attempts.Retries create a new problem: the same event can arrive more than once. For example, your server processes the payment, but its  is lost on the way back. The provider sees a failure and sends the event again.That means handlers should be . Record each event ID you process, and skip any event you have already seen, so a duplicate  does not mark an order as paid twice or trigger a second shipment.async function handlePaymentWebhook(event) {
  // Skip events we have already processed
  if (await processedEvents.has(event.id)) return;

  await markOrderPaid(event.order_id);
  await processedEvents.add(event.id);
}In production, the check and the write should happen in one transaction, or behind a unique constraint on the event ID, so two copies arriving at the same time cannot both get through.Because a webhook endpoint is exposed to the internet, anyone can try to send requests to it. Your server therefore needs a way to verify that each request actually came from the expected sender.A common solution is . The sender and receiver share a secret key. The sender uses that secret to compute an HMAC over the request payload and includes the signature in a header.When the webhook arrives, the receiver computes the HMAC again using the same secret and compares the two signatures. If they do not match, the request is rejected.Many systems also include a timestamp in the signed data. This allows the receiver to reject old requests and helps protect against replay attacks, where someone captures a valid webhook and sends it again later.If you want to go deeper into these topics like how to make Webhooks reliable or how to scale WebSockets across millions of connections, I cover them in much more detail in my  resource at . It includes visual explanations, animations, quizzes, and hands-on exercises to help you apply these concepts in real system design scenarios.
